@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities1
    @title=Declaring Entities
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    [b]What is Entity?[/b]

    In the entity framework an entity is supposed to be ... an entity, in other words a special kind of a class which
    keeps only data and does not have any methods.

    [b]How to Define Entity?[/b]

    The entity which is good for an entity framework must:

    @list
        @list-item
            be declared as a public class
        @end
        @list-item
            have parameterless constructor
        @end
        @list-item
            have entity data declared as public properties with get and set accessors defined.
        @end
    @end

    The entity is attributed using [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.EntityAttribute]EntityAttribute[/clink]. The attribute sets the table name.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
        [Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
        public class Entity
        {
        }
    @end

    [b]How to Define Property?[/b]

    The properties are attributed using [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.EntityPropertyAttribute]EntityPropertyAttribute[/clink].
    In the simplest case nothing additional needs to be set.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty()]
       !    public int IntegerProperty { get; set; }
       !}
    @end

    In this case the property name, type and parameters are all taken from the property definition.

    [b]When To Configure Property Size?[/b]

    For some types the property size cannot be get from the property definition (e.g. for strings) or may be not effective
    if it is taken from the property definition (e.g. numbers).

    In this case [c]Size[/c] and [c]Precision[/c] (for numbers) attribute properties can be utilized.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64)]
       !    public string StringProperty { get; set; }
       !
       !    //8-digit number, 2 digits after decimal point
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 8, Precision = 2)]
       !    public string NumericProperty { get; set; }
       !}
    @end

    [b]When To Set Property Type?[/b]

    For some types the appropriate database type cannot be get from the property definition. For example, C# for not have special types for
    date and timestamps, but the most of database do.

    In this case the DB type needs to be specified.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !       [EntityProperty(DbType=DbType.Date)]
       !       public DateTime DateProperty { get; set; }
       !}
    @end

    [b]How to Make a Property Nullable?[/b]

    To make a property nullable, set [c]Nullable[/c] property of the attribute to [c]true[/c]. Also, if the property type is a
    value type, change the property type to nullable.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !       [EntityProperty(DbType=DbType.Date, Nullable=true)]
       !       public DateTime? DateProperty { get; set; }
       !}
    @end

    [b]How to Add an Index to the Property?[/b]

    To add an index to the property use [c]Sorted[/c] property of the attribute.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !       [EntityProperty(DbType=DbType.Date, Sorted=true)]
       !       public DateTime? DateProperty { get; set; }
       !}
    @end

    [b]How to Customize Database Field Name?[/b]

    If you want to customize the database field name use [c]Field[/c] property of the attribute.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !       [EntityProperty(Field="action_date", DbType=DbType.Date, Sorted=true)]
       !       public DateTime? DateProperty { get; set; }
       !}
    @end
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities2
    @title=Primary Keys
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    The entity framework does not support complex primary keys. Only one property can be a primary key of the entity.

    To mark the property as a primary key use [c]PrimaryKey[/c] property of the attribute. The property of any type except properties
    mapped to BLOB and CLOB columns can be declared as primary key.

    When a property is declared as a primary key, it will be used for:

    @list
        @list-item
            Updating the entity in the database
        @end
        @list-item
            Loading a single instance of the entity from the database
        @end
        @list-item
            Establishing relationship with other entities
        @end
    @end

    For integer artificial primary keys the [c]Autoincrement[/c] property can be used to create an sequence that starts from 1 and increases
    by one every time when a new entity is saved to the database. This operation is atomic at the database level and uniqueness of the values
    of guaranteed.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(PrimaryKey = true, Autoincrement = true)]
       !    public int PK { get; set; }
       !}
    @end

    A shortcut for and integer primary key with autoincrement is [c]AutoId[/c]

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int PK { get; set; }
       !}
    @end

    Please note that autoincrement sequences ALWAYS start from 1 for each entity individually, so you can use [c]PK == 0[/c] condition
    to check whether you are dealing with a new entity.
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities3
    @title=Foreign Keys
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    [b]Refer to Other Table[/b]

    To create a reference to other entity:

    @list
        @list-item
            Create a property that has the type of the other entity.
        @end
        @list-item
            Mark it with [c]EntityProperty[/c] attribute with [c]ForeignKey[/c] property set to [c]true[/c].
        @end
    @end

    In the case, the column with the type of the primary key of the other entity will be added to this table and the foreign key constraint
    will be created.

    When [c]0..*[/c] relationship needs to be established, use [c]Nullable[/c] property of the attribute.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
   ![Entity(Table = "dictionary_table")]
   !public class Dictionary
   !{
   !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
   !    public int ID { get; set; }
   !
   !}
   !
   !
   ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
   !public class Entity
   !{
   !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
   !    public int ID { get; set; }
   !
   !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true, Nullable = true)]
   !    public Dictionary Dictionary { get; set; }
   !}
    @end


    [b]Self-References Table[/b]

    You can make self-reference to the entity in order to create a tree table.

    Please note that:

    @list
        @list-item
            The list reference must ALWAYS be nullable.
        @end
        @list-item
            MySQL does NOT support tree queries, so using tree tables will exclude MySQL and MariaDb from the
            list of the DB that can be used in the application.
        @end
    @end
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_compositeindex
    @title=Compound Indexes
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    A single-column index is created by setting [c]Sorted[/c] on the [c]EntityProperty[/c] attribute.
    To create a [b]compound[/b] (multi-column) index, provide a metadata class that implements
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.Metadata.ICompositeIndexMetadata]ICompositeIndexMetadata[/clink] and
    reference it from the [c]Metadata[/c] property of the
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.EntityAttribute]Entity[/clink] attribute.

    Each index is a [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.Metadata.CompositeIndex]CompositeIndex[/clink] with a
    name and an ordered list of fields. A field can be a plain column, a column with a sort direction,
    or a function applied to a column (an expression index, on the engines that support it).

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "person", Metadata = typeof(PersonMetadata))]
       !public class Person
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64)]
       !    public string LastName { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64)]
       !    public string FirstName { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       !public class PersonMetadata : ICompositeIndexMetadata
       !{
       !    public IEnumerable<CompositeIndex> Indexes
       !    {
       !        get
       !        {
       !            var index = new CompositeIndex(typeof(Person), "byName");
       !            index.Add(nameof(Person.LastName));
       !            index.Add(nameof(Person.FirstName));
       !            yield return index;
       !        }
       !    }
       !}
    @end

    The index is created together with the table. If the set of indexes changes later, the automatic
    schema update reconciles them (see [link=tutorialen_autoupdate]Automatic Schema Update[/link]).

    An index that a particular engine cannot build (for example a functional index on Microsoft SQL
    Server or MySQL) can be excluded from those engines by setting the index's [c]ExcludeFor[/c]
    property to the list of driver names.
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_jsonproperties
    @title=JSON Properties
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    A property can be stored as a JSON document in a single column. Mark it with the
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.JsonEntityPropertyAttribute]JsonEntityProperty[/clink] attribute instead
    of [c]EntityProperty[/c]. Its value — a primitive, an array of primitives, or an object annotated
    with [c]System.Text.Json[/c] — is serialized to a JSON string when the entity is saved and
    deserialized when it is loaded.

    JSON properties are supported on [b]SQLite, PostgreSQL and Oracle 12.2+[/b] only. Creating a JSON
    column on Microsoft SQL Server or MySQL throws an "unsupported" error.

    Use the [c]Nullable[/c] property to allow a [c]null[/c] value (stored as SQL [c]NULL[/c]).

    [b]Indexing values inside the document[/b]

    Individual primitive values inside the document can be indexed with the repeatable
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.JsonIndexAttribute]JsonIndex[/clink]
    attribute. Each index targets one value path (which may reach a value inside a nested object) and
    its type. Supported value types are string, integer, real, boolean and date/time; arrays, binary
    data and whole nested objects are stored but cannot be indexed.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !public class Profile
       !{
       !    public string Name { get; set; }
       !    public int Age { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       ![Entity(Table = "person")]
       !public class Person
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [JsonEntityProperty(Nullable = true)]
       !    [JsonIndex("$.Age", DbType.Int32)]
       !    public Profile Profile { get; set; }
       !}
    @end

    The whole document is read and written automatically with the entity. Individual values inside the
    document can also be filtered, projected, sorted, grouped and aggregated in entity queries - see
    [link=tutorialen_jsonquery]Querying by JSON Properties[/link].
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_jsonquery
    @title=Querying by JSON Properties
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    Besides reading and writing the whole document, individual values inside a
    [link=tutorialen_jsonproperties]JSON property[/link] can be used directly in a query: in the
    [c]WHERE[/c] condition, in the result set (including an aggregate), and in [c]ORDER BY[/c],
    [c]GROUP BY[/c] and [c]HAVING[/c]. A value can be addressed either by a JSON path string (for
    example [c]"$.Address.State"[/c]) or, equivalently, by a member expression such as
    [c]e =&gt; e.Profile.Address.State[/c] - both forms are shown below.

    The examples below use this entity:

    @example
        @title=Entity
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !public class Address
       !{
       !    public string City { get; set; }
       !    public string State { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       !public class Profile
       !{
       !    public string Name { get; set; }
       !    public int Age { get; set; }
       !    public decimal Income { get; set; }
       !    public DateTime DoB { get; set; }
       !    public int[] Scores { get; set; }
       !    public Address Address { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       ![Entity(Table = "person")]
       !public class Person
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [JsonEntityProperty(Nullable = true)]
       !    [JsonIndex("$.Age", DbType.Int32)]
       !    [JsonIndex("$.Address.State", DbType.String)]
       !    public Profile Profile { get; set; }
       !}
    @end

    [b]Filtering[/b]

    In the [c]Where[/c] (or [c]On[/c]) condition of any entity query, start a JSON-value predicate with
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.JsonPropertyConditionBuilderExtension.JsonPropertyOf]JsonPropertyOf&lt;T&gt;[/clink],
    then apply the comparison. The value is addressed either by the property name, JSON path and value
    type, or by a member expression (from which the property, path and value type are inferred). A
    predicate composes with the ordinary [c]And[/c]/[c]Or[/c] and with normal column conditions, and it
    also works with the
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesCountQuery]SelectEntitiesCountQuery[/clink].

    @example
        @title=Example: filter by a JSON value (path form)
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Person>())
       !{
       !    query.Where.JsonPropertyOf<Person>("Profile", "$.Address.State", DbType.String).Eq().Value("CA")
       !               .And().JsonPropertyOf<Person>("Profile", "$.Age", DbType.Int32).Ge().Value(30);
       !    var adults = query.ReadAll<Person>();
       !}
    @end

    The same predicate written with member expressions. The expression may descend into nested objects
    ([c]e =&gt; e.Profile.Address.State[/c]) and index an array element
    ([c]e =&gt; e.Profile.Scores[0][/c]); the value type is taken from the member.

    @example
        @title=Example: filter by a JSON value (expression form)
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Person>())
       !{
       !    query.Where.JsonPropertyOf<Person>(e => e.Profile.Address.State).Eq().Value("CA")
       !               .And().JsonPropertyOf<Person>(e => e.Profile.Age).Ge().Value(30);
       !    var adults = query.ReadAll<Person>();
       !}
    @end

    A row whose JSON value is absent (or [c]null[/c]) simply does not match the predicate.

    [b]Selecting, Ordering, Grouping and Aggregating[/b]

    To use a JSON value as a [i]selected[/i], [i]sortable[/i], [i]groupable[/i] or [i]aggregatable[/i]
    value, use the custom query surface,
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase]SelectEntitiesQueryBase[/clink]
    (obtained via [c]connection.GetSelectEntitiesQueryBase&lt;T&gt;()[/c]). Add the value to the result
    set with
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.AddJsonValueToResultset]AddJsonValueToResultset&lt;T&gt;[/clink];
    an overload wraps it in an aggregate function. The value is read back through the dynamic reader
    ([c]ReadAllDynamic[/c] / [c]ReadOneDynamic[/c]). Every method that takes a property name and path
    also has a member-expression overload.

    @example
        @title=Example: project and order by JSON values (path form)
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQueryBase<Person>())
       !{
       !    query.AddToResultset(typeof(Person), nameof(Person.ID), "id");
       !    query.AddJsonValueToResultset<Person>("Profile", "$.Address.City", DbType.String, "city");
       !    query.AddJsonValueToResultset<Person>("Profile", "$.Income", DbType.Currency, "income");
       !    query.AddJsonValueToOrderBy<Person>("Profile", "$.Income", DbType.Currency, SortDir.Desc);
       !    foreach (dynamic row in query.ReadAllDynamic())
       !    {
       !        string city = row.city;      // null when the path is absent
       !        decimal income = row.income;
       !    }
       !}
    @end

    @example
        @title=Example: the same projection with member expressions
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQueryBase<Person>())
       !{
       !    query.AddToResultset(typeof(Person), nameof(Person.ID), "id");
       !    query.AddJsonValueToResultset<Person>(e => e.Profile.Address.City, "city");
       !    query.AddJsonValueToResultset<Person>(e => e.Profile.Income, "income");
       !    query.AddJsonValueToOrderBy<Person>(e => e.Profile.Income, SortDir.Desc);
       !    var rows = query.ReadAllDynamic();
       !}
    @end

    A JSON value can be added to [c]GROUP BY[/c]
    ([clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.AddJsonValueToGroupBy]AddJsonValueToGroupBy&lt;T&gt;[/clink])
    and to [c]ORDER BY[/c]
    ([clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.AddJsonValueToOrderBy]AddJsonValueToOrderBy&lt;T&gt;[/clink]);
    an aggregate is added to the result set with the [c]AggFn[/c] overload of
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.AddJsonValueToResultset]AddJsonValueToResultset&lt;T&gt;[/clink].
    A [c]HAVING[/c] condition on an aggregated JSON value is started with [c]JsonPropertyOf&lt;T&gt;[/c] on
    the query's [c]Having[/c] builder and wrapped in the aggregate function.

    @example
        @title=Example: sum grouped by a JSON value with a filtered aggregate
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQueryBase<Person>())
       !{
       !    query.AddJsonValueToResultset<Person>(e => e.Profile.Address.State, "state");
       !    query.AddJsonValueToResultset<Person>(AggFn.Sum, e => e.Profile.Income, "total");
       !    query.AddJsonValueToGroupBy<Person>(e => e.Profile.Address.State);
       !    query.Having.JsonPropertyOf<Person>(e => e.Profile.Income).Sum().Gt().Value(1000000m);
       !    query.AddJsonValueToOrderBy<Person>(e => e.Profile.Address.State);
       !    var groups = query.ReadAllDynamic();
       !}
    @end

    [b]Notes[/b]

    @list
        @list-item
            The value type must be a primitive: string, an integer type, real/decimal, boolean or
            date/time. Whole nested objects and arrays cannot be extracted (but a single array element,
            such as [c]"$.Scores[0]"[/c] / [c]e =&gt; e.Profile.Scores[0][/c], can).
        @end
        @list-item
            A [c]DateTime[/c] value is stored and indexed as an ISO-8601 string on all three databases,
            so filter and sort it as [c]DbType.String[/c] and compare against an ISO-8601 string
            ([c]"2000-01-31T00:00:00"[/c]); ISO-8601 text sorts chronologically. In the expression form,
            pass the type explicitly to override the one inferred from the member:
            [c]JsonPropertyOf&lt;Person&gt;(e =&gt; e.Profile.DoB, DbType.String)[/c].
        @end
        @list-item
            When a JSON path is indexed with [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.JsonIndexAttribute]JsonIndex[/clink],
            extracting or filtering that path with the [i]same[/i] type lets the database use the index.
        @end
        @list-item
            These expression overloads belong to the entity query surface; JSON values cannot yet be used
            inside [link=tutorialen_linq]entity LINQ collection[/link] lambdas.
        @end
    @end
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities4
    @title=Queries
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    There are a few entity queries that can be created using
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.EntityConnectionExtension]EntityConnectionExtension[/clink] extension class.

    The methods of this class creates one of
    subclasses of [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.EntityQuery]EntityQuery[/clink] classes. An entity query is
    a disposable object and must be disposed after use and before the connection to which the query belongs is disposed.

    An entity queries can be executed using method [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.EntityQuery.Execute.4MF]Execute[/clink].

    Depending on the query it may have a parameter if the query operates on a single instance of the entity (e.g. insert, update or delete).
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities5
    @title=Creating and Dropping Tables
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    [b]Dropping and Creating an Individual Table[/b]

    There are entity queries to drop and to create entity tables. See example below for both available ways to create an entity query:

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs

       !//Drop query: Option 1
       !using (var query = connection.GetDropEntityQuery<Entity>())
       !    query.Execute();
       !//Drop query: Option 2
       !using (var query = connection.GetDropEntityQuery(typeof(Entity)))
       !    query.Execute();
       !//Create query: Option 1
       !using (var query = connection.GetCreateEntityQuery<Entity>())
       !    query.Execute();
       !//Create query: Option 2
       !using (var query = connection.GetCreateEntityQuery(typeof(Entity)))
       !    query.Execute();
    @end

    Please note that the order, in which tables are dropped or created, matters. In case, say, you have an entity B that refers via
    a foreign key to an entity B, you have to drop B entity first and you have to create an entity A first.

    [b]Using Entity Creation Controller[/b]

    To simplify tables management, there are the class [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.CreateEntityController]CreateEntityController[/clink]
    provided. The class loads all entities (or all entities filtered by a [i]scope[/i] specified) from the assembly and then orders them
    according relationships between assemblies. Then you can drop all entities or create all entities using just one call.

    Of course, in case when you want to recreate all the entity tables, always drop entities first.

    Please note that if you want your view automatically created and dropped, don't forget to
    provide metadata type in the [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.EntityAttribute]EntityAttribute[/clink]
    that implements [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.Metadata.IViewCreationMetadata]IViewCreationMetadata[/clink]
    interface.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs

        CreateEntityController controller = new CreateEntityController(typeof(Entity).Assembly);
        controller.DropTables(connection);
        controller.CreateTables(connection);
    @end

    [b]Updating Entities[/b]

    Often, during the application lifecycle, the database structure are changes to reflect new requirements and functionality. Not always
    full recreation of the database is the right answer, sometime updates are the option.

    There are two typical ways on how the application can handle this situation:

    @list
        @list-item
            Provide the update script that prepares the database for running a new version.
        @end
        @list-item
            Use automatic update procedure,
            provided by [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.CreateEntityController]CreateEntityController[/clink]
            class (see [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.CreateEntityController.UpdateTables.Z93]Update()[/clink] method).
        @end
    @end

    To update the database in the second way:

    @list
        @list-item
            Create new entities and add new properties to the entities as usual.
        @end
        @list-item
            Mark entities that won't be used anymore with [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.ObsoleteEntityAttribute]ObsoleteEntity[/clink] attribute.
        @end
        @list-item
            Mark entity properties that won't be used anymore with [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.ObsoleteEntityPropertyAttribute]ObsoleteEntityProperty[/clink] attribute.
        @end
        @list-item
            If additional actions is required when entity is changed (created, dropped) or property is changed (created, dropped) use
            the appropriate attribute that calls a static method that takes just one parameter - a connection. On drop actions are performed before
            the operation and on create are performed after.
            The attributes are:
            @list
                @list-item
                    [c]OnEntityCreate[/c]
                @end
                @list-item
                    [c]OnEntityDrop[/c]
                @end
                @list-item
                    [c]OnEntityPropertyCreate[/c]
                @end
                @list-item
                    [c]OnEntityPropertyDrop[/c]
                @end
            @end
        @end
        @list-item
            Be aware of automatic updates limitations. The automatic update cannot:
            @list
                @list-item
                    Change type, name or attributes of an existing property.
                @end
                @list-item
                    Add or remove primary key of an existing entity.
                @end
                @list-item
                    Create a new non-nullable property. All newly introduced properties must be nullable.
                @end
            @end
        @end
    @end

    @example
        @title=Example: Using update attributes
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
         ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
         ![b][OnEntityCreate(typeof(MyClass), nameof(MyClass.OnEntityCreate))][/b]
         !public class Entity
         !{
         !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
         !    public int ID { get; set; }
         !
         !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true, Nullable = true)]
         !    public Dictionary Dictionary { get; set; }
         !
         !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64, Nullable = true)]
         !    [b][OnEntityPropertyCreate(typeof(MyClass), nameof(MyClass.OnEntityPropertyCreate))][/b]
         !    public string MyNewProperty { get; set; }
         !}
         !
         !public class MyClass
         !{
         !    [b]public static void OnEntityCreate(SqlDbConnection connection)[/b]
         !    {
         !
         !    }
         !
         !    [b]public static void OnEntityPropertyCreate(SqlDbConnection connection)[/b]
         !    {
         !
         !    }
         !
    @end
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_autoupdate
    @title=Automatic Schema Update
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    As shown in the previous topic,
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.CreateEntityController]CreateEntityController[/clink]
    can bring an existing database in line with the current entity definitions without recreating it.
    This topic describes what the automatic update does and how to control it.

    [b]What the Automatic Update Does[/b]

    When you call the controller's [c]UpdateTables[/c] method against an existing database, it:

    @list
        @list-item
            creates a table for an entity that does not exist yet;
        @end
        @list-item
            adds a column for a newly added property (new properties must be nullable);
        @end
        @list-item
            drops a table or a column marked obsolete (see the previous topic);
        @end
        @list-item
            reconciles the table indexes with the entity definition (see below).
        @end
    @end

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs

        !CreateEntityController controller = new CreateEntityController(typeof(Entity).Assembly);
        !controller.UpdateTables(connection, CreateEntityController.UpdateMode.Update);
    @end

    [b]Index Reconciliation[/b]

    During the update the controller also keeps the table indexes in sync with the entity definition.
    For every existing table it:

    @list
        @list-item
            creates an index that is declared on the entity but missing in the database;
        @end
        @list-item
            drops an index it previously created whose declaration was removed from the entity;
        @end
        @list-item
            drops and recreates an index whose set of columns has changed.
        @end
    @end

    Two kinds of index are reconciled:

    @list
        @list-item
            single-column indexes declared with [c]EntityProperty(Sorted = true)[/c] (and the
            automatic foreign-key indexes);
        @end
        @list-item
            compound indexes declared through
            [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.Metadata.ICompositeIndexMetadata]ICompositeIndexMetadata[/clink]
            / [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.Metadata.CompositeIndex]CompositeIndex[/clink].
        @end
    @end

    Adding, dropping or changing a [c]UNIQUE[/c] or [c]PRIMARY KEY[/c] constraint is [b]not[/b]
    handled by the automatic update, because those are emitted as inline column constraints rather
    than as standalone indexes. Change such a constraint by recreating the table
    ([c]UpdateMode.Recreate[/c]).

    [b]Excluding an Index on Specific Databases[/b]

    A functional (expression) index cannot be created directly on the engines that do not allow
    functions in indexes (Microsoft SQL Server and MySQL); by default such an index throws an
    exception there. To declare, explicitly, that an index must be skipped on a given engine, set the
    [c]ExcludeFor[/c] property of the
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.Metadata.CompositeIndex]CompositeIndex[/clink] to the list of driver
    names (see [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.UniversalSqlDbFactory]UniversalSqlDbFactory[/clink]).

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs

        !var index = new CompositeIndex(typeof(Document), "byTitle")
        !{
        !    ExcludeFor = new[] { UniversalSqlDbFactory.MSSQL, UniversalSqlDbFactory.MYSQL }
        !};
        !index.Add(SqlFunctionId.Upper, nameof(Document.Title));
    @end

    On an excluded engine the index is silently skipped and is created on all others. Adding an engine
    to [c]ExcludeFor[/c] for an index that already exists there causes the next update to drop it on
    that engine.

    [b]Important: Do Not Reuse the Framework Index Naming[/b]

    The framework names every index it creates [i]table[/i]_[i]index[/i], and it uses exactly that
    convention to recognise which indexes it owns. During reconciliation any index whose name starts
    with the table name followed by an underscore, and which is not part of the current entity
    definition, is treated as an orphaned framework index and is [b]dropped[/b].

    Therefore, when you create an index manually (outside the entity definition), [b]do not[/b] name it
    using the [i]table[/i]_[i]name[/i] pattern - give it a distinct name (for example [c]ix_manual_...[/c]).
    Primary-key and unique-constraint indexes are never affected, because the database identifies them
    as unique or primary regardless of their name.
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities6
    @title=Inserting, Updating and Deleting Entities
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    There are [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.ModifyEntityQuery]ModifyEntityQuery[/clink] that can be used
    to create, update or delete a single instance of the entity.

    This query is the only query that requires the instance of the entity to be specified as a
    parameter of the [c]Execute[/c] method.

    The using of the query is quite simple. Just get the query using
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.EntityConnectionExtension]EntityConnectionExtension[/clink] extension class
    and call it for the instance. In case multiple entities must be handled, the same query may be reused multiple times.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
           !using (var query = connection.GetInsertEntityQuery<Entity>())
           !    query.Execute(entity);
           !
           !using (var query = connection.GetUpdateEntityQuery<Entity>())
           !    query.Execute(entity);
           !
           !using (var query = connection.GetDeleteEntityQuery<Entity>())
           !    query.Execute(entity);
    @end

    If the primary key of the entity is integer autoincrement value, you can use universal approach that detects whether
    the entity needs to be updated or it needs to be inserted.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
        !    using (var query = entity.ID < 1 ? connection.GetInsertEntityQuery<Entity>() : connection.GetUpdateEntityQuery<Entity>())
        !        query.Execute(entity);
    @end
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities7
    @title=Selecting Entities Count and Selecting All Entities
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    [b]Get Entities Count[/b]

    To get number of entities use entity count query:

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesCountQuery<Entity>())
       !    countOf = query.RowCount;
    @end


    [b]Read All Entities[/b]

    To read all entities (with looking up for the whole tree of foreign keys references):

    @example
        @title=Example
        @highlight=cs
        @show=yes
        !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>())
        !{
        !   EntityCollection<Entity> all = query.ReadAll<Entity>();
        !   ...
        !}
    @end

    If you don't want to read all entities at once but want to handle them one-by-one:

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
        !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>())
        !{
        !   Entity entity;
        !   while ((entity = query.ReadOne<Entity>()) != null)
        !   {
        !       ......
        !   }
        !}
    @end
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities8
    @title=Filtering and Ordering Query Results
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    Select queries (and, by the way, multiple entity modification and deletion queries) can be applied
    to the filtered rows only.

    To do it [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.ConditionEntityQueryBase.Where.ty7]Where[/clink]
    property is used.

    There are a lot of options how the resultset can be filtered. Let's discuss major options of filtering.

    [b]Adding Simple Compare Conditions[/b]

    The easiest way to filter is to simply compare a column value with a constant value.

    The column is simply referenced by the property name. C# [c]nameof[/c] operation is very useful to specify the property names, so, the chance
    of mistype is reduced and no complex and boring replace operation would be needed if ever the property is renamed.

    You can compare to all kind of equality comparison: is equal, is not equal, is greater, is greater or equal, is less, is less or equal.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
        ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
        !public class Entity
        !{
        !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
        !    public int ID { get; set; }
        !
        !    [EntityProperty]
        !    public bool UpdateStatistics { get; set; }
        !
        !    [EntityProperty(Nullable = true)]
        !    public int? Statistics { get; set; }
        !}
        !..................
        !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>())
        !{
        !    //select only rows where statistics is equal to zero
        !    query.Where.Property(nameof(Entity.Statistics)).Is(CmpOp.Eq).Value(0);
        !    //or select rows where statistics is between 20 and 100
        !    query.Where.Property(nameof(Entity.Statistics)).Is(CmpOp.Ge).Value(20);
        !    query.Where.And().Property(nameof(Entity.Statistics)).Is(CmpOp.Le).Value(100);
        !
        !}
    @end

    [b]Is Null/Is Not Null Conditions[/b]

    Is null/is not null conditions are even simpler as they do not require parameters:

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
        ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
        !public class Entity
        !{
        !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
        !    public int ID { get; set; }
        !
        !    [EntityProperty]
        !    public bool UpdateStatistics { get; set; }
        !
        !    [EntityProperty(Nullable = true)]
        !    public int? Statistics { get; set; }
        !}
        !..................
        !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>())
        !{
        !    //select only rows where statistics is not set
        !    query.Where.Property(nameof(Entity.Statistics)).Is(CmpOp.IsNull);
        !
        !}
        @end

    [b]Add Conditions for Referenced Entities[/b]

    The default entity query loads not only the main entity, but all referenced entities as well. These entities can also be used
    in the filter. All we need is to add the type of the entity referenced.

    Let say we need to select entities that have referenced to dictionary entries that starts from the letter A.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "dictionary_table")]
       !public class Dictionary
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64, Sorted = true)]
       !    public string Name { get; set; }
       !
       !}
       !
       !
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true)]
       !    public Dictionary Reference { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       !....
       !
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    query.Where.PropertyOf<Dictionary>(nameof(Dictionary.Name)).Is(CmpOp.Like).Value("A%");
       !    var collection = query.ReadAll<Entity>();
       !}
    @end


    [b]Subquery Conditions (IN)[/b]

    Another query can be used as a part of the query reference. Here we are using the information that will be provided in further articles (on how
    to customize the resultset of an entity query), however, the examples below are quite simple and pretty much self-explaining.

    Let say we need to select entities that have referenced to dictionary entries that starts from the letter A.

    In this particular case this is just another (and less effective!) solution of the problem demonstrated in the previous example, however,
    this method can be used for custom queries which do not have references resolved or to
    more complex dictionary filtering, e.g. to select all dictionaries that have at least 10 entities referenced to them.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @highlight=cs
        @show=yes
       ![Entity(Table = "dictionary_table")]
       !public class Dictionary
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64, Sorted = true)]
       !    public string Name { get; set; }
       !
       !}
       !
       !
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true)]
       !    public Dictionary Reference { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       !....
       !
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    using (var subquery = connection.GetGenericSelectEntityQuery<Dictionary>())
       !    {
       !        subquery.AddToResultset(nameof(Dictionary.ID));
       !        subquery.Where.Property(nameof(Dictionary.Name)).Is(CmpOp.Like).Value("A%");
       !
       !        query.Where.Property(nameof(Entity.Reference)).Is(CmpOp.In).Query(subquery);
       !    }
       !
       !    var collection = query.ReadAll<Entity>();
       !}
    @end

    [b]Subquery Conditions With Cross-Reference[/b]

    Sometimes subquery needs to be referenced to the parent query. Like in example below where we need to find all dictionary entries
    that do not have any entities related to them. The cross references may be established using the result returned by
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.ConditionEntityQueryBase.GetReference]GetReference()[/clink] method.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "dictionary_table")]
       !public class Dictionary
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64, Sorted = true)]
       !    public string Name { get; set; }
       !
       !}
       !
       !
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true)]
       !    public Dictionary Reference { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       !....
        !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Dictionary>())
        !{
        !    using (var subquery = connection.GetGenericSelectEntityQuery<Entity>())
        !    {
        !        subquery.Where.Property(nameof(Entity.Reference)).Is(CmpOp.Eq).Reference(query.GetReference(nameof(Dictionary.ID)));
        !        query.Where.Add().Is(CmpOp.NotExists).Query(subquery);
        !    }
        !
        !    var unusedDictionaryEntries = query.ReadAll<Dictionary>();
        !}
    @end

    [b]Put Conditions into Bracket and Joining With OR[/b]

    Sometimes conditions needs to be joined by OR instead of AND (i.e. ANY of the conditions is true rather than ALL of the conditions are true)

    Example below selects all rows where statistics is not set or is less than 100.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
        !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>())
        !{
        !    using (var bracket = query.Where.AddGroup())
        !    {
        !        query.Where.Or().Property(nameof(Entity.Statistics)).Is(CmpOp.IsNull);
        !        query.Where.Or().Property(nameof(Entity.Statistics)).Is(CmpOp.Le).Value(100);
        !    }
        !
        !}
    @end

    Sometimes, OR and AND conditions needs to be mixed. You must be aware that AND is always used first, before OR. In such cases the developer
    may want to enclose a set of conditions into a group in sake of predictable behavior and better readability of the query. The brackets needs to
    be used in this case. The principles of usage are example the same as using the brackets in the plain SQL or any other programming language.

    The example below is equal to raw SQL [c]updateStatistics AND (statistics is NULL OR (statistics >= 20 AND statistics <= 180)[/c]

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
           !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>())
           !{
           !
           !    query.Where.Property(nameof(Entity.UpdateStatistics)).Is(CmpOp.Eq).Value(true);
           !    using (var bracket = query.Where.AddGroup(LogOp.And))
           !    {
           !        query.Where.Or().Property(nameof(Entity.Statistics)).Is(CmpOp.IsNull);
           !        using (var bracket1 = query.Where.AddGroup(LogOp.Or))
           !        {
           !            query.Where.Property(nameof(Entity.Statistics)).Is(CmpOp.Ge).Value(20);
           !            query.Where.And().Property(nameof(Entity.Statistics)).Is(CmpOp.Le).Value(180);
           !        }
           !    }
           !}
    @end

    [b]Ordering Resultset[/b]

    To order the resultset use [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.AddOrderBy]AddOrderBy()[/clink]
    method of the query. The data can be ordered by any column of any entity involved into the query.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    //order by the name of the referenced dictionary entry
       !    query.AddOrderBy(typeof(Dictionary), nameof(Dictionary.Name));
       !    //and then by statistics in reverse order
       !    query.AddOrderBy(nameof(Entity.Statistics), SortDir.Desc);
       !    query.Limit = 20;
       !    query.Skip = 100;
       !    ...
       !}
    @end


    [b]Limiting Rows[/b]

    To limit the number of rows received or to skip rows from the beginning of the query use
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.Limit.yWG]Limit[/clink]
    and [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.Skip.c83]Skip[/clink] properties
    of the query. The example below reads 20 entities starting at 100th entity.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    query.AddOrderBy(typeof(Dictionary), nameof(Dictionary.Name));
       !    query.Limit = 20;
       !    query.Skip = 100;
       !    ...
       !}
    @end


    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    query.AddOrderBy(typeof(Dictionary), nameof(Dictionary.Name));
       !    query.Limit = 20;
       !    query.Skip = 100;
       !    ...
       !}
    @end

    Please be aware that some of database requires the resultset to be ordered to use limit and skip features.
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities9
    @title=Limit Which Columns are Selected (the filter method).
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    Sometimes, especially when the entity consists of large data such as clob or blob, it is reasonable to skip loading of
    this data when large amount of entities is received.

    It is possible to do not load some columns while the entity is still loaded with all references resolved and is bound to
    the entity object using column filters.

    The column filter is passed to the method of obtaining of the select query. The column filter simply lists the entity type and the
    column name to be skipped.

    Skipped nullable properties will be filled with null values. Skipped non-nullable properties will be filled with default values of
    the property type.

    In the example below, the entity property "Statistics" and the referenced property aren't loaded, however can be referenced in
    query filter.

    The reference field may also be filtered, however this will affect only loading the data. The referenced entity will be included into the query
    and so it may be used in filters and order specification.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "dictionary_table")]
       !public class Dictionary
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64, Sorted = true)]
       !    public string Name { get; set; }
       !
       !}
       !
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true)]
       !    public Dictionary Reference { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Nullable = true)]
       !    public int? Statistics { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       !...
       !
       !SelectEntityQueryFilter[] filter = new SelectEntityQueryFilter[]
       !{
       !    new SelectEntityQueryFilter() {Property = nameof(Entity.Statistics)},
       !    new SelectEntityQueryFilter() {EntityType = typeof(Dictionary), Property = nameof(Dictionary.Name)},
       !};
       !
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>(filter))
       !{
       !    query.Where.PropertyOf<Dictionary>(nameof(Dictionary.Name)).Is(CmpOp.Like).Value("A%");
       !    var all = query.ReadAll<Entity>();
       !}
    @end
@end


@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities10
    @title=Limit Which Columns are Selected (the generic query method) and Aggregating Queries.
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    In case query need to produce custom resultset and custom entity query, a generic entity select query needs to be used. The generic entity select
    class is [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase]SelectEntitiesQueryBase[/clink]

    [b]Defining Resultset[/b]

    The generic entity query does not have any resultset defined when the object is created.

    Use [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.AddToResultset]AddToResultset()[/clink] method to specify which columns
    needs to be returned.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "dictionary_table")]
       !public class Dictionary
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64, Sorted = true)]
       !    public string Name { get; set; }
       !
       !}
       !
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true)]
       !    public Dictionary Reference { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Nullable = true)]
       !    public int? Statistics { get; set; }
       !}
       !....
       !using (var query = connection.GetGenericSelectEntityQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    query.AddToResultset(nameof(Entity.ID));
       !    query.AddToResultset(nameof(Entity.Statistics));
       !    ....
       !}
    @end

    [b]Reading Resultset[/b]

    There are two way to read the result of generic entity query.

    First option is to read the resultset row-by-row and access each individual column using [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.EntityQuery.GetValue]GetValue()[/clink]
    method.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !....
       !using (var query = connection.GetGenericSelectEntityQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    query.AddToResultset(nameof(Entity.ID));
       !    query.AddToResultset(nameof(Entity.Statistics));
       !    while (query.ReadNext())
       !    {
       !        int id = query.GetValue<int>(0);
       !        int stasitics = query.GetValue<int>(1);
       !    }
       !
       !}
    @end

    The second option is to use dynamic objects. In this case it would be reasonable to explicitly
    define the names of the columns as they will be used as property names of the dynamic object.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !....
       !using (var query = connection.GetGenericSelectEntityQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    query.AddToResultset(nameof(Entity.ID), "id");
       !    query.AddToResultset(nameof(Entity.Statistics), "stat");
       !
       !    var rs = query.ReadAllDynamic();
       !    foreach (var row in rs)
       !    {
       !        int id = row.id;
       !        int stat = row.stat;
       !    }
       !}
    @end


    [b]Aggregating the Data[/b]

    The data in the generic query may be aggregated. Use [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.AggFn]AggFn[/clink] function id while adding the data
    to the resultset.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true)]
       !    public Dictionary Reference { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Nullable = true)]
       !    public int? Statistics { get; set; }
       !}
       !....................
       !using (var query = connection.GetGenericSelectEntityQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !     query.AddToResultset(AggFn.Max, nameof(Entity.Statistics), "max");
       !     var rs = query.ReadOneDynamic()?.max;
       !}
    @end

    When the data needs to be aggregated by some value, use [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.AddGroupBy]AddGroupBy[/clink]
    method. Some database requires all columns from the resultset that aren't aggregate function to be included into
    group by clause. The framework automatically does this if at least one non-aggregated column exists in the resultset.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "subentity_table")]
       !public class Subentity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true, Nullable = true)]
       !    public Entity Entity { get; set; }
       !
       !}
       !....
       !using (var query = connection.GetGenericSelectEntityQuery<Subentity>())
       !{
       !    query.AddToResultset(nameof(Subentity.Entity), "entity_id");
       !    query.AddToResultset(AggFn.Count, null, "count");
       !    query.AddGroupBy(nameof(Subentity.Entity));
       !    var rs = query.ReadAllDynamic();
       !}
    @end

    [b]Joins[/b]

    Generic select by default has only one entity table involved, which was set when the query is created.

    You can add more entities using [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.AddEntity]AddEntity[/clink] method.

    The framework automatically recognizes the type of join (inner, left or right) using primary and foreign key information in the entities.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "dictionary_table")]
       !public class Dictionary
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64, Sorted = true)]
       !    public string Name { get; set; }
       !
       !}
       !
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true)]
       !    public Dictionary Reference { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Nullable = true)]
       !    public int? Statistics { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       !...
       !using (var query = connection.GetGenericSelectEntityQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    query.AddEntity(typeof(Dictionary));
       !    query.AddToResultset(nameof(Entity.ID));
       !    query.AddToResultset(typeof(Dictionary), nameof(Dictionary.Name));
       !    var rs = query.ReadAllDynamic();
       !}
    @end

    In rare cases, especially when one entity is referenced in the other more than once, it requires to explicitly specify which column
    is used to join and which occurrence of the type in the query needs to be used in result set and/or the filter.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "dictionary_table")]
       !public class Dictionary
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64, Sorted = true)]
       !    public string Name { get; set; }
       !
       !}
       !
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true)]
       !    public Dictionary Reference { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true)]
       !    public Dictionary SecondReference { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Nullable = true)]
       !    public int? Statistics { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       !...
       !using (var query = connection.GetGenericSelectEntityQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    query.AddEntity(typeof(Dictionary), nameof(Entity.Reference), false);
       !    query.AddEntity(typeof(Dictionary), nameof(Entity.SecondReference), true);

       !    query.AddToResultset(nameof(Entity.ID));
       !    query.AddToResultset(typeof(Dictionary), nameof(Dictionary.Name), "firstname");
       !    query.AddToResultset(typeof(Dictionary), 1, nameof(Dictionary.Name), "secondname");

       !    var rs = query.ReadAllDynamic();
       !}
    @end
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities16
    @title=LINQ Methods of Queries
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    The LINQ extension class [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.Linq.EntityQueryLinqExtension]EntityQueryLinqExtension[/clink]
    extends entity queries with ability to use LINQ expressions instead of types and names in resultset, filter and grouping specifications.

    In most cases using of the LINQ expressions does not affect generated SQL quality and performance, however the expression compilation itself is
    slower than basic methods. The effect is not significant and can be ignored in applications that are not time or resource critical.


    Sometimes the filters also may look simpler when specified via LINQ expression.

    Let's look how we can alternatively specify the filtering condition (the entities will be the same for all examples in this article).

    Let say we need to select entities that have value between 15 and 30 or that are lined to dictionary record with the name dict5.

    First query in the example uses conventional approach to define the filter and the second query uses a LINQ expression for the same purpose.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "dictionary")]
       !public class Dictionary
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64, Sorted = true)]
       !    public string Name { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       ![Entity(Table = "entity")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true)]
       !    public Dictionary Reference { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Sorted = true)]
       !    public int Value { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64, Sorted = true)]
       !    public string Name { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       !....
       !//Conventional query
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesCountQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !
       !    query.Where.PropertyOf<Dictionary>(nameof(Dictionary.Name)).Is(CmpOp.Eq).Value("dict5");
       !    using (var b = query.Where.AddGroup(LogOp.Or))
       !    {
       !        query.Where.Property(nameof(Entity.Value)).Is(CmpOp.Gt).Value(15);
       !        query.Where.Property(nameof(Entity.Value)).Is(CmpOp.Ls).Value(30);
       !    }
       !    int rc = query.RowCount;
       !}
       !
       !
       !//Equivalent LINQ based query
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesCountQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    query.Where.And().Expression<Entity, Dictionary>((entity, dictionary) => (entity.Value > 15 && entity.Value < 30) || dictionary.Name == "dict5");
       !    int rc = query.RowCount;
       !}
    @end

    LINQ expressions offers a few options which are not available for regular queries, such as:

    @list
        @list-item
            It is possible to set custom join expression in [c]AddEntity[/c] method.
        @end
        @list-item
            It is possible to add an expression into resultset.
        @end
    @end

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !double coefficient = 11.2;
       !
       !using (var query = connection.GetGenericSelectEntityQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    query.AddEntity(typeof(Dictionary));
       !    query.AddToResultset<Entity, int>(e => e.ID, "id");
       !    query.AddToResultset<Entity, string>(e => e.Name, "ent_id");
       !    query.AddToResultset<Dictionary, string>(d => d.Name, "dict_name");
       !    query.AddToResultset<Entity, Dictionary, string>((e, d) => SqlFunction.Upper(SqlFunction.Concat(e.Name, "-", d.Name)), "name");
       !    query.AddToResultset<Entity, double>(e => e.Value * coefficient, "value");
       !
       !    dynamic rs = query.ReadAllDynamic();
       !}
    @end

    Pay attention that where function is used, a function from class [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.Linq.SqlFunction]SqlFunction[/clink] may
    only be used. The same class is also used to specify aggregate functions,
    when the operator does not exist in the C# language (e.g. like, in, exists) or when reference to
    the value in parent query needs to be used.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !//select entities that have value above the average value of all entities
       !//associated with the same dictionary record
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Entity>())
       !{
       !    using (var subquery = connection.GetGenericSelectEntityQuery<Entity>())
       !    {
       !        subquery.AddToResultset<Entity, double>(entity => SqlFunction.Avg(entity.Value));
       !        subquery.Where.Expression<Entity>(entity => SqlFunction.Value<int>(subquery.GetReference(nameof(Entity.Reference))) == SqlFunction.Value<int>(query.GetReference(nameof(Entity.Reference))));
       !        query.Where.Expression<Entity>(entity => entity.Value >= SqlFunction.Value<double>(subquery) && SqlFunction.Like(entity.Reference.Name, "%0%"));
       !    }
       !
       !    query.Execute();
       !    var rs = query.ReadAll<Entity>();
       !}
    @end

    You can also have limited access to the entity using LINQ queries to a IQueriable collection of an entity.
    Use [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.Linq.QueryableEntityProvider]QueryableEntityProvider[/clink] to get such collection.

    Only the following methods are supported: from, group, order and select.

    Below there are few examples of LINQ queries. Again it is positively advised against using true LINQ queries, especially in connection with
    the grouping operation because of poor performance of compiled SQL queries.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
           !QueryableEntityProvider provider = new QueryableEntityProvider(new QueryableEntityProviderConnection(connection));
           !
           !var query = from e in provider.Entities<Entity>()
           !    where e.Value > 10
           !    select e;
           !
           !Entity[] rs = query.ToArray();
           !
           !var query1 = from e in provider.Entities<Entity>()
           !    group e by e.Reference.ID
           !    into g
           !    select new {DictRec = g.Key, MaxValue = g.Max(v => v.Value)};
           !
           !var rs1 = query1.ToArray();
    @end
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_linq
    @title=Using an Entity Collection with LINQ
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    Besides using [link=tutorialen_entities16]LINQ expressions inside a regular query[/link], the framework can expose an
    entity as an [c]IQueryable&lt;T&gt;[/c] collection that you query with ordinary LINQ - [c]Where[/c], [c]Select[/c],
    [c]OrderBy[/c], [c]GroupBy[/c], the aggregates, [c]First[/c] and so on. The whole LINQ expression is translated into a
    single SQL [c]SELECT[/c] and executed on the server.

    [b]A Word of Caution[/b]

    LINQ is convenient for simple, ad-hoc queries, but it is [b]not[/b] the recommended way to write performance-critical
    ones. LINQ semantics do not map perfectly onto the SQL [c]SELECT[/c] structure, so - as with any LINQ-to-SQL provider -
    the generated SQL is rarely as good as a query written by hand against the
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase]SelectEntitiesQueryBase[/clink] surface; grouping in
    particular tends to compile into sub-optimal SQL. Prefer the manual query surface on hot paths and reach for LINQ where
    the convenience outweighs the cost.

    [b]Getting the Collection[/b]

    Call [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.Linq.EntityQueryLinqConnectionExtension.GetCollectionOf.ki5]GetCollectionOf&lt;T&gt;[/clink]
    on a connection to obtain a [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.Linq.QueryableEntity]QueryableEntity&lt;T&gt;[/clink],
    which is an [c]IQueryable&lt;T&gt;[/c]. (The lower-level
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.Linq.QueryableEntityProvider]QueryableEntityProvider[/clink] can also be used
    directly, but the connection extension is the simplest entry point.) The examples below use the [c]Entity[/c] and
    [c]Dictionary[/c] classes from the [link=tutorialen_entities16]previous article[/link].

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !var entities = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>();
    @end

    [b]Filtering[/b]

    Use [c]Where[/c] with the usual C# operators ([c]&amp;&amp;[/c], [c]||[/c], comparisons). Where C# has no operator for a
    SQL function (e.g. [c]LIKE[/c]), call the corresponding method on
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.Linq.SqlFunction]SqlFunction[/clink]. Navigation across a foreign key works too.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !var inRange = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>()
       !    .Where(e => e.Value > 15 && e.Value < 30)
       !    .ToList();
       !
       !var byName = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>()
       !    .Where(e => SqlFunction.Like(e.Name, "item%") || e.Reference.Name == "dict5")
       !    .ToList();
    @end

    [b]Selecting[/b]

    Without a [c]Select[/c] the whole entity is returned. A [c]Select[/c] projects into an anonymous type (a custom result
    set) or into a single scalar value.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !// whole entities
       !var all = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>().ToList();
       !
       !// projection into an anonymous type
       !var rows = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>()
       !    .Select(e => new { e.ID, e.Name, e.Value })
       !    .ToList();
       !
       !// a single scalar
       !var names = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>()
       !    .Select(e => e.Name)
       !    .ToList();
    @end

    [b]Ordering and Grouping[/b]

    [c]OrderBy[/c] sorts the result; [c]GroupBy[/c] groups it, and the group projection may reference the key ([c]g.Key[/c])
    and aggregates over the group.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !var ordered = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>()
       !    .OrderBy(e => e.Value)
       !    .ToList();
       !
       !var perDictionary = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>()
       !    .GroupBy(e => e.Reference.ID)
       !    .Select(g => new { DictId = g.Key, MaxValue = g.Max(v => v.Value) })
       !    .ToList();
    @end

    [b]Aggregates and Paging[/b]

    [c]Count[/c] (optionally with a predicate) and [c]Average[/c] (and the other aggregates) execute on the server. An
    aggregate over the whole collection can also be written as a [c]Select[/c] of an
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.Linq.SqlFunction]SqlFunction[/clink] aggregate followed by [c]First[/c].
    [c]First[/c] / [c]FirstOrDefault[/c] read a single row, and [c]Skip[/c] / [c]Take[/c] page the result.

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !int count = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>().Count(e => e.Value > 15);
       !double average = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>().Average(e => e.Value);
       !int max = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>().Select(e => SqlFunction.Max(e.Value)).First();
       !
       !var firstByName = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>().OrderBy(e => e.Name).First();
       !var secondPage = connection.GetCollectionOf<Entity>().OrderBy(e => e.ID).Skip(20).Take(10).ToList();
    @end

    [b]Limitations[/b]

    @list
        @list-item
            The supported operators are [c]Where[/c], [c]Select[/c], [c]OrderBy[/c], [c]GroupBy[/c], [c]Count[/c],
            [c]Sum[/c], [c]Min[/c], [c]Max[/c], [c]Average[/c], [c]First[/c], [c]FirstOrDefault[/c], [c]Skip[/c] and [c]Take[/c].
        @end
        @list-item
            Only ascending [c]OrderBy[/c] is translated; [c]OrderByDescending[/c] and [c]ThenBy[/c] are not supported.
        @end
        @list-item
            Compare against a local variable or a literal, not a [c]static[/c] field - a static-field reference cannot be
            compiled into the query.
        @end
        @list-item
            Any function used inside an expression must come from
            [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.Linq.SqlFunction]SqlFunction[/clink] (or a supported [c]string[/c] /
            [c]DateTime[/c] member), so that it is guaranteed to run on the server.
        @end
    @end
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities18
    @title=Using plain SQL on entities.
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    [b]SQL Queries on Entities[/b]

    In some cases the job to be performed is more complex that supported by the current implementation of the
    entity framework. In this case you can fallback to plain SQL (or to use an SQL builder).

    To get the SQL table related to the entity:

    @list
        @list-item
            Find the entity descriptor using [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.AllEntities]AllEntities[/clink] class.
        @end
        @list-item
            Get the table descriptor using [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.EntityDescriptor.TableDescriptor.I5A]TableDescriptor[/clink] property
            of the entity descriptor.
        @end
    @end

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "subentity_table")]
       !public class Subentity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(ForeignKey = true, Nullable = true)]
       !    public Entity Entity { get; set; }
       !
       !}
       !
       ![Entity(Table = "entity_table")]
       !public class Entity
       !{
       !    [EntityProperty(AutoId = true)]
       !    public int ID { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty]
       !    public int Statistics { get; set; }
       !}
       !
       !
       !//.................
       !//update statistics in all entities where statistics is zero
       !//using subquery to calculate number of subentities for each entity updated
       !TableDescriptor entityTable = AllEntities.Inst[typeof(Entity)].TableDescriptor;
       !TableDescriptor subentityTable = AllEntities.Inst[typeof(Subentity)].TableDescriptor;
       !
       !UpdateQueryBuilder updateBuilder = new UpdateQueryBuilder(connection.GetLanguageSpecifics(), entityTable);
       !
       !SelectQueryBuilder selectQueryBuilder = new SelectQueryBuilder(connection.GetLanguageSpecifics(), subentityTable);
       !selectQueryBuilder.AddToResultset(AggFn.Count);
       !selectQueryBuilder.Where.Property(subentityTable[nameof(Subentity.Entity)]).Is(CmpOp.Eq).Raw(updateBuilder.GetAlias(entityTable[nameof(Entity.ID)]));
       !
       !updateBuilder.AddUpdateColumnSubquery(entityTable[nameof(Entity.Statistics)], selectQueryBuilder);
       !updateBuilder.Where.Property(entityTable[nameof(Entity.Statistics)]).Is(CmpOp.Eq).Parameter("param1");
       !
       !using (SqlDbQuery query = connection.GetQuery(updateBuilder))
       !{
       !    query.BindParam("param1", 0);
       !    query.ExecuteNoData();
       !}
    @end

    Note: You can always check what is the plain SQL select query by checking [c]SelectBuilder.SelectQueryBuilder.Query[/c] at your query
    AFTER your entity query is executed.

    [b]SQL Queries on the result of an entity query[/b]

    You can always apply a plain SQL select query on the results of entity select query. To add the entity select query result as a table to
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.QueryBuilder.SelectQueryBuilder]SelectQueryBuilder[/clink], use
    [c]SelectBuilder.SelectQueryBuilder.QueryTableDescriptor[/c] property of your query. (NOTE: You do not NEED to execute your query, it will be executed
    as a subquery of the plain SQL select query that you will construct).

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ! //MyEntity (*) --- (1) MyDictionary
       ! //Let's count average count of entities per dictionary record
       !using (SelectEntitiesQueryBase subquery = connection.GetGenericSelectEntityQuery<MyEntity>())
       !{
       !    subquery.AddEntity<MyDictionary>();
       !    subquery.AddToResultset(typeof(MyDictionary), nameof(MyDictionary.ID), "dictid");
       !    subquery.AddToResultset(typeof(MyDictionary), nameof(MyDictionary.Name), "dictname");
       !    subquery.AddToResultset(AggFn.Count, null, "count");
       !    subquery.AddGroupBy(typeof(MyDictionary), nameof(MyDictionary.ID));
       !
       !    SelectQueryBuilder builder = new SelectQueryBuilder(connection.GetLanguageSpecifics(), subquery.SelectBuilder.SelectQueryBuilder.QueryTableDescriptor);
       !    builder.AddToResultset(AggFn.Avg, subquery.SelectBuilder.SelectQueryBuilder.QueryTableDescriptor["count"]);
       !    using (SqlDbQuery query = connection.GetQuery(builder))
       !    {
       !            query.ExecuteReader();
       !            query.ReadNext();
       !            return query.GetValue<int>(0);
       !    }
       !}
    @end
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_dynentity
    @title=Dynamic Entities
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    [b]What is a Dynamic Entity?[/b]

    An ordinary entity is a compiled class - its table name and its set of columns are fixed at compile time. A
    [b]dynamic entity[/b] describes the same information [i]at run time[/i] instead: the table and the list of properties
    (columns) are produced by code rather than by fixed class members. This is useful when the schema is known only at run
    time - for example a table described by configuration, or a per-tenant table.

    A dynamic entity is, in every other respect, a normal entity: it is created, dropped, inserted, updated, deleted and
    queried exactly like a compiled one.

    [b]How to Define a Dynamic Entity?[/b]

    Derive from [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.DynamicEntity]DynamicEntity[/clink] and override two members:

    @list
        @list-item
            the [c]EntityAttribute[/c] property - it returns the [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.EntityAttribute]EntityAttribute[/clink]
            that names the table (the same attribute a compiled entity would carry);
        @end
        @list-item
            the [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.DynamicEntity.InitializeProperties.dDF]InitializeProperties[/clink] method -
            it returns the list of properties, each described by a
            [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.DynamicEntityProperty]DynamicEntityProperty[/clink] (a CLR type, a name and an
            [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.EntityPropertyAttribute]EntityPropertyAttribute[/clink] - exactly the information the
            [c]EntityProperty[/c] attribute carries for a compiled entity).
        @end
    @end

    @example
        @title=Example: defining a dynamic entity
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !public class Product : DynamicEntity
       !{
       !    public override EntityAttribute EntityAttribute
       !        => new EntityAttribute() { Scope = "catalog", Table = "product" };
       !
       !    protected override IEnumerable<IDynamicEntityProperty> InitializeProperties()
       !    {
       !        yield return new DynamicEntityProperty(typeof(int), "Id",
       !            new EntityPropertyAttribute() { PrimaryKey = true, Autoincrement = true });
       !        yield return new DynamicEntityProperty(typeof(string), "Name",
       !            new EntityPropertyAttribute() { Size = 64, Nullable = true });
       !        yield return new DynamicEntityProperty(typeof(double), "Price",
       !            new EntityPropertyAttribute());
       !    }
       !}
    @end

    [b]How to Use a Dynamic Entity?[/b]

    Pass the dynamic entity type to the ordinary entity queries. Because the class derives from [c]DynamicObject[/c], the
    property values are read and written dynamically (through a [c]dynamic[/c] reference).

    @example
        @title=Example: creating the table and inserting a row
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetCreateEntityQuery(typeof(Product)))
       !    query.Execute();
       !
       !dynamic product = new Product();
       !product.Name = "Widget";
       !product.Price = 9.99;
       !
       !using (var query = connection.GetInsertEntityQuery(typeof(Product)))
       !    query.Execute((object)product);
    @end

    [b]Discovery[/b]

    Dynamic entities are [b]not[/b] discovered by the entity finder automatically. If you rely on entity discovery, call
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.AllEntities.EnableDynamicEntityDiscoveryInEntityFinder.k26]AllEntities.EnableDynamicEntityDiscoveryInEntityFinder()[/clink]
    once at start-up to enable it.
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_dynprops1
    @title=Dynamic (Per-Row) Properties
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    [b]What are Dynamic Properties?[/b]

    Regular entity properties are fixed by the class definition - every row has exactly the same columns.
    [b]Dynamic properties[/b] let an individual row carry additional, named values that are [i]not[/i] part of the
    class - a flat bag of name/value pairs that may differ from one row to the next, without any schema change.

    They are useful for user-defined fields, sparse attributes, or data whose shape is not known at compile time.

    [b]Why Do You Need Them?[/b]

    Their main purpose is to [b]avoid a schema change for small or infrequent additions to the model[/b]. Adding a
    regular column means altering the table (a migration) and changing the entity class; for a handful of optional or
    rarely-used fields - especially ones that only some rows need, or that are defined by the user rather than by the
    developer - that overhead is rarely justified. A dynamic property stores such a value without touching the table
    structure or changing the entity.

    Good candidates are optional metadata, user-defined fields, feature flags and other sparse attributes. If a value is
    present on (almost) every row and is central to the model, a regular column is still the better choice.

    [b]Not the Same as Dynamic Entities![/b]

    Despite the similar name, dynamic properties have nothing to do with [link=tutorialen_dynentity]dynamic entities[/link].
    A dynamic entity defines a whole table's schema at run time, but every row of that table still has the same fixed set of
    columns. Dynamic properties are the opposite idea: extra, [i]per-row[/i] values layered on top of an entity, where
    different rows may carry different names. The two features are independent - and can even be combined (a dynamic entity
    may also own dynamic properties).

    [b]How are They Stored?[/b]

    The values are kept in a separate [i]side table[/i], one per entity table, named [c]&lt;entity_table&gt;_props[/c]
    (the suffix is configurable). Each value is stored in its own row together with the owner's primary key and the
    property name, so no change to the entity table itself is ever required. The layout is plain SQL and works on
    every supported driver.

    The side table is created and dropped [b]automatically[/b] together with the entity table - the ordinary
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.EntityConnectionExtension.GetCreateEntityQuery]GetCreateEntityQuery[/clink] and
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.EntityConnectionExtension.GetDropEntityQuery]GetDropEntityQuery[/clink] calls
    take care of it. You do not create it yourself.

    [b]How to Enable Dynamic Properties on an Entity?[/b]

    Mark the entity class with the [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.DynamicPropertiesAttribute]DynamicProperties[/clink] attribute
    and implement the [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.IDynamicPropertiesOwner]IDynamicPropertiesOwner[/clink]
    interface. The interface exposes the bag as a read-only property backed by a private setter (the framework fills it
    in via reflection when the properties are loaded).

    @example
        @title=Example
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       ![Entity(Table = "document")]
       ![DynamicProperties]
       !public class Document : IDynamicPropertiesOwner
       !{
       !    [AutoId]
       !    public int Id { get; set; }
       !
       !    [EntityProperty(Size = 64, Nullable = true)]
       !    public string Name { get; set; }
       !
       !    public DynamicPropertyBag DynamicProperties { get; private set; }
       !}
    @end

    The [c]DynamicProperties[/c] attribute also carries optional tuning:
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.DynamicPropertiesAttribute.NameSize.fE2]NameSize[/clink] (default 64),
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.DynamicPropertiesAttribute.StringValueSize.mdB]StringValueSize[/clink] (default 256) and the
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.DynamicPropertiesAttribute.TableSuffix.sl7]TableSuffix[/clink] used for the side table (default [c]_props[/c]).

    [b]Which Value Types are Supported?[/b]

    A dynamic property value may be one of the following types:

    @list
        @list-item
            [c]string[/c]
        @end
        @list-item
            [c]int[/c] and [c]long[/c]
        @end
        @list-item
            [c]double[/c]
        @end
        @list-item
            [c]bool[/c]
        @end
        @list-item
            [c]DateTime[/c] (stored as UTC)
        @end
    @end

    [b]How to Set and Get Values?[/b]

    The values live in the [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.DynamicPropertyBag]DynamicPropertyBag[/clink]. For a brand-new entity,
    call [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.DynamicPropertiesExtension.InitializeDynamicProperties.x1B]InitializeDynamicProperties()[/clink]
    to create an empty bag; then use [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.DynamicPropertyBag.Set.R3F]Set[/clink] to assign values by name.
    The bag also offers [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.DynamicPropertyBag.Get]Get[/clink], the typed [c]Get&lt;T&gt;[/c],
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.DynamicPropertyBag.Contains.LnA]Contains[/clink],
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.DynamicPropertyBag.Remove.CpA]Remove[/clink] and
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.DynamicPropertyBag.Count.P04]Count[/clink].

    @example
        @title=Example: creating an entity with dynamic properties
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !var document = new Document() { Name = "contract" };
       !var bag = document.InitializeDynamicProperties();
       !bag.Set("color", "red");
       !bag.Set("pages", 12);
       !bag.Set("archived", false);
       !bag.Set("signed_at", DateTime.UtcNow);
       !
       !using (var query = connection.GetInsertEntityQuery<Document>())
       !    query.Execute(document);
    @end

    [b]When are the Values Saved?[/b]

    The bag is saved [b]automatically[/b] whenever the owning entity is inserted or updated with the ordinary
    entity modify queries - there is no separate save call. The bag tracks its own changes, so an update writes only
    the values that were added, changed or removed and leaves the rest untouched. Deleting the entity removes its
    dynamic property rows as well.

    [b]How to Load the Values Back?[/b]

    Dynamic properties are [b]not[/b] loaded automatically when an entity is read (that would cost an extra query per
    row). There are two ways to load them:

    @list
        @list-item
            Set [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQuery.PreloadProperties.ON7]PreloadProperties[/clink] to
            [c]true[/c] on a [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQuery]SelectEntitiesQuery[/clink]
            before reading. The properties for the whole result set are then loaded in batches and attached to each entity.
        @end
        @list-item
            Call [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.EntityConnectionExtension.LoadPropertiesFor.ei1]connection.LoadPropertiesFor(entity)[/clink]
            to load (or reload) the bag of a single entity you already have.
        @end
    @end

    @example
        @title=Example: reading entities together with their dynamic properties
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Document>())
       !{
       !    query.PreloadProperties = true;
       !    foreach (var document in query.ReadAll<Document>())
       !    {
       !        string color = document.DynamicProperties.Get<string>("color");
       !        //...
       !    }
       !}
    @end

    [b]Performance Considerations[/b]

    Dynamic properties trade query performance for schema flexibility. Each value is a separate row in the side table, so
    reading or filtering by a property costs a join (or a sub-query) against that table rather than a plain column read.
    The side table is indexed by name and value, so looking up or filtering a [i]single[/i] property is efficient, but:

    @list
        @list-item
            If you need to [b]search, sort or aggregate by a field extensively[/b] (or join on it), make it a regular
            entity property - a real column - not a dynamic one. A native column with its own index will always outperform
            an EAV lookup.
        @end
        @list-item
            Reading many properties for a row means reading many side-table rows; prefer [c]PreloadProperties[/c] (which
            batches the load for the whole result set) over loading each entity individually.
        @end
        @list-item
            A value that exists on nearly every row is cheaper and simpler as a regular column.
        @end
    @end

    In short: use dynamic properties for sparse, occasionally-queried, user- or configuration-driven data; promote a value
    to a real column as soon as it becomes "hot" - frequently searched, sorted or joined.

    See [link=tutorialen_dynprops2]Querying by Dynamic Properties[/link] for how to filter, sort, group and aggregate by a dynamic property.
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_dynprops2
    @title=Querying by Dynamic Properties
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    Once an entity owns [link=tutorialen_dynprops1]dynamic properties[/link], you can filter, sort, group and aggregate
    by them in queries.

    [b]How to Filter by a Dynamic Property?[/b]

    In the [c]Where[/c] condition of any entity query, start a dynamic-property predicate with
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.EntityQueryConditionBuilderDynamicPropertiesExtension.DynamicPropertyOf]DynamicPropertyOf[/clink],
    passing the owner entity type and the property name, then apply the comparison. The value column and its type are
    inferred from the value you compare against.

    @example
        @title=Example: filter by a dynamic property
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Document>())
       !{
       !    query.Where.DynamicPropertyOf<Document>("color").Eq("red");
       !    var red = query.ReadAll<Document>();
       !}
    @end

    Dynamic-property predicates compose with the ordinary [c]And[/c]/[c]Or[/c] and with normal column conditions, and
    they also work with [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesCountQuery]SelectEntitiesCountQuery[/clink].
    A row whose property is absent (or was stored under a different type) simply does not match the predicate.

    @example
        @title=Example: combining conditions
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQuery<Document>())
       !{
       !    query.Where.Property(nameof(Document.Name)).Is(CmpOp.Like).Value("contract%")
       !               .And().DynamicPropertyOf<Document>("pages").Ge(10);
       !    var found = query.ReadAll<Document>();
       !}
    @end

    [b]Projecting, Sorting, Grouping and Aggregating[/b]

    To use a dynamic property as a [i]selected[/i], [i]sortable[/i], [i]groupable[/i] or [i]aggregatable[/i] value, use the
    custom query surface, [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase]SelectEntitiesQueryBase[/clink]
    (obtained via [c]connection.GetSelectEntitiesQueryBase(...)[/c]).

    Because these clauses have no value to infer the type from, the value type is specified explicitly with the
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.DynamicPropertyValueType]DynamicPropertyValueType[/clink] enumeration
    ([c]String[/c], [c]Integer[/c], [c]Long[/c], [c]Real[/c], [c]Boolean[/c], [c]DateTime[/c]). Add the property to the
    result set with [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.AddDynamicPropertyToResultset]AddDynamicPropertyToResultset[/clink];
    the value is returned already decoded to the declared type.

    @example
        @title=Example: selecting a dynamic property
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQueryBase<Document>())
       !{
       !    query.AddToResultset(typeof(Document), nameof(Document.Name), "name");
       !    query.AddDynamicPropertyToResultset<Document>("color", DynamicPropertyValueType.String, "color");
       !    foreach (dynamic row in query.ReadAllDynamic())
       !    {
       !        string name = row.name;
       !        string color = row.color;   // null when the row has no "color"
       !    }
       !}
    @end

    An aggregate overload wraps the value in an aggregate function.

    @example
        @title=Example: aggregating a dynamic property
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQueryBase<Document>())
       !{
       !    query.AddDynamicPropertyToResultset<Document>(AggFn.Max, "pages", DynamicPropertyValueType.Integer, "max_pages");
       !    dynamic row = query.ReadOneDynamic();
       !    int maxPages = row.max_pages;
       !}
    @end

    [b]Ordering, Grouping and Having[/b]

    A dynamic property can be added to [c]ORDER BY[/c]
    ([clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.AddDynamicPropertyToOrderBy]AddDynamicPropertyToOrderBy[/clink]),
    [c]GROUP BY[/c]
    ([clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.AddDynamicPropertyToGroupBy]AddDynamicPropertyToGroupBy[/clink])
    and [c]HAVING[/c]
    ([clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.SelectEntitiesQueryBase.HavingDynamicPropertyOf]HavingDynamicPropertyOf[/clink]),
    but [b]only after it has been added to the result set[/b] (with the same name, type and occurrence) - the query reuses
    that projection. Adding one of these for a property that was not projected throws an exception.

    @example
        @title=Example: group by a dynamic property with a filtered aggregate
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !using (var query = connection.GetSelectEntitiesQueryBase<Document>())
       !{
       !    query.AddDynamicPropertyToResultset<Document>("color", DynamicPropertyValueType.String, "color");
       !    query.AddToResultset(AggFn.Count, typeof(Document), nameof(Document.Id), "count");
       !    query.AddDynamicPropertyToGroupBy<Document>("color", DynamicPropertyValueType.String);
       !    query.HavingDynamicPropertyOf<Document>("pages", DynamicPropertyValueType.Integer).Sum().Gt(100);
       !    query.AddDynamicPropertyToOrderBy<Document>("color", DynamicPropertyValueType.String);
       !    var groups = query.ReadAllDynamic();
       !}
    @end

    [b]A Note on Efficiency[/b]

    When a dynamic property is both projected and used in the [c]Where[/c] condition, the query filters the already-joined
    value directly instead of issuing an additional sub-query - so projecting a property you also filter on costs nothing extra.
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_dynprops3
    @title=Dynamic Properties in LINQ Queries
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    [link=tutorialen_dynprops1]Dynamic properties[/link] can also be queried through the
    [link=tutorialen_linq]entity LINQ collection[/link], as an alternative to the
    [link=tutorialen_dynprops2]manual query surface[/link]. Inside any LINQ lambda, read a dynamic property with
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Entities.DynamicPropertyBag.Get.UG2]Get&lt;T&gt;[/clink] on the entity's [c]DynamicProperties[/c] bag:
    the same [c]e.DynamicProperties.Get&lt;T&gt;("name")[/c] expression works in [c]Where[/c], in a [c]Select[/c] projection,
    inside an aggregate, and in [c]OrderBy[/c] / [c]GroupBy[/c]. The type argument [c]T[/c] selects which value the property
    is read as. The examples below use the [c]Document[/c] entity from
    [link=tutorialen_dynprops1]Dynamic (Per-Row) Properties[/link].

    [b]Filtering[/b]

    @example
        @title=Example: filter by a dynamic property
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !var red = connection.GetCollectionOf<Document>()
       !    .Where(e => e.DynamicProperties.Get<string>("color") == "red")
       !    .ToList();
       !
       !var big = connection.GetCollectionOf<Document>()
       !    .Where(e => e.DynamicProperties.Get<int>("pages") >= 10 &&
       !               e.DynamicProperties.Get<bool>("archived") == false)
       !    .ToList();
    @end

    Filtering uses SQL [c]NULL[/c] semantics: a row whose property is absent matches neither [c]==[/c], [c]!=[/c] nor a range
    comparison. Use [c]== null[/c] to select the rows that do not have the property.

    [b]Selecting[/b]

    A projected dynamic property is returned already decoded to [c]T[/c]. For a value type that may be [i]absent[/i] on some
    rows, project into the nullable form ([c]int?[/c], [c]DateTime?[/c], ...) so an absent value reads as [c]null[/c].

    @example
        @title=Example: project dynamic properties
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !var rows = connection.GetCollectionOf<Document>()
       !    .Select(e => new
       !    {
       !        e.Name,
       !        Color = e.DynamicProperties.Get<string>("color"),   // null when absent
       !        Pages = e.DynamicProperties.Get<int?>("pages")      // null when absent
       !    })
       !    .ToList();
    @end

    [b]Aggregating[/b]

    Wrap the property read in an [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.Linq.SqlFunction]SqlFunction[/clink] aggregate.

    @example
        @title=Example: aggregate a dynamic property
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !int maxPages = connection.GetCollectionOf<Document>()
       !    .Select(e => SqlFunction.Max(e.DynamicProperties.Get<int>("pages")))
       !    .First();
    @end

    [b]Ordering and Grouping[/b]

    @example
        @title=Example: order and group by a dynamic property
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !var byColor = connection.GetCollectionOf<Document>()
       !    .Where(e => e.DynamicProperties.Get<string>("color") != null)
       !    .OrderBy(e => e.DynamicProperties.Get<string>("color"))
       !    .ToList();
       !
       !var counts = connection.GetCollectionOf<Document>()
       !    .GroupBy(e => e.DynamicProperties.Get<string>("color"))
       !    .Select(g => new { Color = g.Key, Count = g.Count() })
       !    .ToList();
    @end

    [b]Reading Whole Entities with the Bag Attached[/b]

    When a whole-entity query (one without a [c]Select[/c]) returns entities that own dynamic properties, the bag is loaded
    and attached to each entity [b]by default[/b], so you can read the values straight off the returned objects. Pass
    [c]preloadDynamicProperties: false[/c] to
    [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.Linq.EntityQueryLinqConnectionExtension.GetCollectionOf.ki5]GetCollectionOf[/clink]
    to skip the extra load when you do not need the bag.

    @example
        @title=Example: whole entities with (and without) the bag
        @show=yes
        @highlight=cs
       !// the bag is attached to every returned entity (default)
       !foreach (var doc in connection.GetCollectionOf<Document>().Where(e => e.Name == "contract"))
       !{
       !    string color = doc.DynamicProperties.Get<string>("color");
       !}
       !
       !// opt out of the extra property load
       !var lite = connection.GetCollectionOf<Document>(preloadDynamicProperties: false).ToList();
    @end

    [b]Notes[/b]

    @list
        @list-item
            All six value types are supported ([c]string[/c], [c]int[/c], [c]long[/c], [c]double[/c], [c]bool[/c] and
            [c]DateTime[/c]); [c]bool[/c] and [c]DateTime[/c] are stored in an encoded form and are compared and returned
            transparently.
        @end
        @list-item
            As with any LINQ query here, compare against a local variable or a literal, not a [c]static[/c] field.
        @end
    @end
@end

@article
    @key=tutorialen_entities19
    @title=Other useful entity related things
    @brief=
    @ingroup=tutorialsen

    Pay also attention at following features of the framework that aren't discussed above:

    @list
        @list-item
        [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityGenericAccessor]EntityGenericAccessor[/clink] and
        [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityGenericAccessor.GenericEntityAccessorWithAggregates]GenericEntityAccessorWithAggregates[/clink]
        classes that provides basic CRUD operations for entities.
        @end
        @list-item
        [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.MultiUpdateEntityQuery]MultiUpdateEntityQuery[/clink] and
        [clink=Gehtsoft.EF.Db.SqlDb.EntityQueries.MultiDeleteEntityQuery]MultiDeleteEntityQuery[/clink] queries.
        @end
        @list-item
        Limited Bson and MongoDB support.
        @end
    @end
@end